 page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. I'll not add extra keys; instead I can append to number? No — keep number clean. The revision info could go in revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"3\"}]. I'll include revision_history with a single entry revision \"3\" and omit date/changes (not present). That respects schema shape.\n    \"project_number\": \"3800938\",\n    \"page_total\": 9,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\"name\": \"NMi\"},\n  \"recommendation\": {\"id\": \"R117\", \"edition\": 2007, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [0.3, 0.5, 1, 1.5]},\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Mass flow meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"CMF200y\",\"CMF300y\",\"CMF350y\",\"CMF400y\",\"CMFHC2y\",\"CMFHC3y\",\"CMFHC4y\",\"MVD700\",\"MVD800\",\"MVD820\",\"MVD1700\",\"MVD2500\",\"MVD2700\",\"MVD3500\",\"MVD3700\",\"800 Remote Dual Core Processor\",\"5700\"],\n    \"description\": \"Coriolis mass flow measurement sensors of the CMF and CMFHC series used with MVD series electronics (core processors) and 5700 electronics. 'y' is a letter indicating the construction material of the sensor.\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"CMF200y\", \"attributes\": {\"series\": \"CMF\"}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"CMF300y\", \"attributes\": {\"series\": \"CMF\"}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"CMF350y\", \"attributes\": {\"series\": \"CMF\", \"qmin_depends_on_material\": true}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"CMF400y\", \"attributes\": {\"series\": \"CMF\", \"qmin_depends_on_serial_number\": [\"(i)\",\"(ii)\",\"(iii)\"]}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"CMFHC2y\", \"attributes\": {\"series\": \"CMFHC\"}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"CMFHC3y\", \"attributes\": {\"series\": \"CMFHC\"}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"CMFHC4y\", \"attributes\": {\"series\": \"CMFHC\"}}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [\n    {\"role\": \"measurement sensor\", \"type_designations\": [\"CMF200y\",\"CMF300y\",\"CMF350y\",\"CMF400y\",\"CMFHC2y\",\"CMFHC3y\",\"CMFHC4y\"], \"alternatives\": null},\n    {\"role\": \"electronics / core processor\", \"type_designations\": [\"MVD700 Core Processor\",\"MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor\",\"MVD820\",\"MVD1700\",\"MVD2500\",\"MVD2700\",\"MVD3500\",\"MVD3700\",\"800 Remote Dual Core Processor\",\"5700\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\"}\n  ],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"model_level\": [...],\n    \"config_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...],\n  \"footnotes\": [...]\n}\n\nWait — environmental classes config_level: condition uses numbers \"700, 800, ...\" — in the context \"MVD series electronics\". I'll write conditions \"MVD700, MVD800, MVD820, MVD1700, MVD2700, MVD3500, MVD3700\" and \"MVD2500\".\n\nAmbient temp: \"Ambient temperature range: -40… +55 °C; condensing humidity\". Extract:\nconfig_level: {attribute: \"ambient_temperature_range\", axis: \"electronics_model\", values: [{condition: \"all MVD series electronics\", value: {min: -40, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}]}\nand {attribute: \"humidity\", axis: \"electronics_model\", values: [{condition: \"all MVD series electronics\", value: \"condensing\"}]}\n\nPower supply as above with axis \"power_supply_option\", conditions \"option_1\", \"option_2\", \"option_3\".\n\nActually maybe cleaner conditions: \"DC (fixed)\", \"DC\", \"DC\", \"AC\". Let me use:\nvalues: [\n  {condition: \"option_1 (DC)\", value: 24, unit_symbol: \"V\"},\n  {condition: \"option_2 (DC)\", value: {min: 18, max: 30}, unit_symbol: \"V\"},\n  {condition: \"option_3 (DC)\", value: {min: 18, max: 100}, unit_symbol: \"V\"},\n  {condition: \"option_3 (AC)\", value: {min: 85, max: 265}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n]\nfrequency: [{condition: \"option_3 (AC)\", value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}]\n\nAxis: \"power_supply_option\".\n\nFootnotes list:\n\n[\n  {\"marker\": \"y\", \"text\": \"y is a letter which indicates the type of material the meter is built of. Where there are different possibilities for a characteristic, the actual letter is indicated in superscript.\"},\n  {\"marker\": \"(i)\", \"text\": \"CMF400: Serial number up to 411000.\"},\n  {\"marker\": \"(ii)\", \"text\": \"CMF400: Serial number from 411000 up to 14200000.\"},\n  {\"marker\": \"(iii)\", \"text\": \"CMF400: Serial number higher than 14200000.\"},\n  {\"marker\": \"(◇)\", \"text\": \"Qmin in combination with the MVD700 Core Processor.\"},\n  {\"marker\": \"(◇◇)\", \"text\": \"Qmin in combination with the MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor and/or 5700 internal core processor.\"},\n  {\"marker\": \"*)\", \"text\": \"Software versions for the 1700 / 2700 which do not have a checksum.\"},\n  {\"marker\": \"**)\", \"text\": \"Software versions for the 2500 which do not have a checksum.\"}\n]\n\nNow write matrix_tables fully.\n\nTable 1 (page 5 top, continuation of CMF sensor table). Name: I need a name; source title not visible in fragment. I'll use \"Specific characteristics of the CMF series measurement sensors (CMF200y, CMF300y, CMF350y, CMF400y)\". description: \"Continuation of sensor characteristics table (page 5). Superscript letters (e.g. A, B, C, E / H, L, M, P) indicate sensor material variants; (i)/(ii)/(iii) denote CMF400 serial number ranges.\"\n\nTable 2: name: \"Table 3: Specific characteristics of the CMFHC series of measurement sensors\". Since source says \"Table 3 Specific characteristics of the CMFHC series of measurement sensors\", name: \"Specific characteristics of the CMFHC series of measurement sensors\".\n\nColumns: [\"attribute\", \"core_processor\", \"accuracy_class\", \"cmfhc2y\", \"cmfhc3y\", \"cmfhc4y\"], column_units: [null, null, null, \"kg/min\", \"kg/min\", \"kg/min\"].\n\nRows:\n- {attribute: \"maximum_flow_rate\", core_processor: null, accuracy_class: null, cmfhc2y: 12600, cmfhc3y: 22000, cmfhc4y: 30000}\n- min flow MVD700: 0.3: 1136/2268/3400; 0.5: 568/1134/1700; 1.0: 284/567/850; 1.5: 227/453/680\n- min flow MVD800: 0.3: 491/1059/1661; 0.5: 245/530/830; 1.0: 123/265/415; 1.5: 123/213/332\n- {attribute: \"minimum_measured_quantity\", ..., 1000, 1000, 1000}\n- {attribute: \"maximum_pressure\", ..., 25, 25, 25}\n\nTable 3: software versions. Name: \"Software versions and checksums of the MVD series electronics\". Columns: [\"processor\", \"version\", \"checksum\"]. column_units: [null, null, null]. rows as enumerated.\n\nAlso mention Table 6 header \"General characteristics of the 5700 electronics\" appears but content not in fragment — could note in description of software table? Better: skip; the fragment ends at that heading. Maybe add to components characteristics? I'll add a mention in certified_type description? Not necessary. I'll skip (rule: content present only). Actually the heading IS present; but no content. I could add a matrix_table placeholder? No. Skip.\n\nLet me now double-check the \"800 Remote Dual Core Processor\" checksums: \"1.00 → 52FB 1CF0\", yes.\n\nAlso the note about the first table being for CMF (Table 2 presumably): given page 4 likely had \"Table 2 Specific characteristics of the CMF series...\" and page 5 continues. Fine.\n\nOne more check on the config_level conditions for CMF350y materials: superscripts \"A, B, C, E\" and \"H, L, M, P\". So condition strings: \"model=CMF350y; core_processor=MVD700; accuracy_class=0.3; materials=A,B,C,E\". For MVD800 CMF350y no superscripts shown — single values.\n\nCMF400y MVD800: only (iii) values given (1000(iii), 500(iii), 250(iii)) — meaning only serial range (iii)? The table shows only (iii) for MVD800. So conditions \"serial_number>14200000\". OK.\n\nSerial range condition text: \"(i): serial number up to 411000\", \"(ii): serial number from 411000 up to 14200000\", \"(iii): serial number higher than 14200000\". I'll encode: \"model=CMF400y; core_processor=MVD700; accuracy_class=0.3; serial_number=up to 411000\". \n\nNow, one concern: the accuracy_class label \"1.0; 1.5\" — keep as \"1.0; 1.5\" for CMF table rows, but in config conditions I'll wri